citizenship_name_central-sphere = Native of the Central Sphere
citizenship_description_central-sphere = The Central Sphere grew around Alpha Centauri A b and counts time from 0 [textlink="GS" link="GuideEntryGS"], the first extrasolar colony. Early years proved one thing: bluespace scrambles comms and stable jumps, so without a shared time scale and archives, colonies drift into different versions of the present. By 12 [textlink="GS" link="GuideEntryGS"] Alpha anchored sync centers and data vaults, and distant news and orders traveled by couriers. The first colonies rode colonial embryo ships—banks of embryos and automation—and only later did Alpha pull scattered colonies together with common rules and time.

    Today the Central Sphere is a network of terraformed worlds with ideal conditions and expensive comfort: clean air, mild climate, antigrav and gardens. Behind the showcase sits familiar control: clearances, cameras, security, contract service, and the habit of not asking questions. The higher the sector, the less you see how many people keep it running.

    Policy is hard centralization around Alpha and its procedures; routes, standards, archival confirmations, and key clearances converge in the capital hubs, and without them the outer worlds hit paperwork. Orders and audits arrive late, so locals decide on the spot and later reconcile to Alpha protocols. Corporations fight for exceptions and priority, but every dispute runs through federal signatures.

    The economy leans on flows from outside: food, raw materials, tech, and labor contracts. In return the Center grants access—routes, certificates, the right to use equipment and data. Money matters, but clearances and reputation matter more: they open doors faster than any sum. Luxury feels conditional: you have it while you're useful and don't cause trouble.

citizenship_name_second-sphere = Native of the Second Sphere
citizenship_description_second-sphere = The Second Sphere was once a showcase of frontier tech: domed cities built to Central standards with redundant life support, automation, and complex logistics. Distance turned that into a trap: domes demanded constant maintenance and rare parts, while long jumps delayed data and orders. Control shifted local—clans claimed warehouses and fuel, the Federation posted curators and archives to keep at least the paperwork. Every shipment and crew now pass two checkpoints: official and the one that actually decides.

    Today the Sphere is a stretched market: faded domes, shops on old frames, docks with endless queues. Rules change from planet to planet; corporate banners feel stronger than distant federal seals. Housing and safety depend on whose contract covers the sector. People live on short routes and local swaps because long deliveries slip. Politics is barter: the Center funds hubs and demands audits, clans hold crews and stock, corporations patch supply gaps and buy priority on the lanes. Checks become races of signatures, and decisions sit with whoever controls the warehouse and comms. The economy is repair and assembly: raw and worn gear arrives from the frontiers, is restored, and sent back out. Cash matters, but a dock slot, fast inspection, and a working line matter more. Those who show results before the auditor arrives win the contract; the rest wait for the next convoy and adjust to the shifted schedules.
